README

This sample program uses a Raspberry Pi with two Ethernet ports as a raw SOEM
(Simple Open EtherCAT Master) master for a single Elmo Platinum servo drive.
The drive is armed and disarmed remotely, over a ZeroMQ broker on the second
Ethernet port, instead of running any built-in demo motion. The EtherCAT/CiA
402 drive control runs on its own CPU core, isolated from the ZeroMQ client.

	+-------------------+  eth0 (raw EtherCAT frames)  +--------------+
	|  Raspberry Pi       |----------------------------->| Elmo Platinum|
	|  core 1: EtherCAT   |                              | drive        |
	|  + CiA 402 (SOEM,   |                              +--------------+
	|    no scheduler)    |
	|                     |
	|  core 0: CScheduler |  eth1 (TCP/IP, ZeroMQ)       +--------------+
	|  (ZeroMQ client)    |----------------------------->| ZeroMQ       |
	+-------------------+                                | broker       |
	                                                      +--------------+

	!!! THIS PROGRAM CAN MOVE A MOTOR, once armed over ZeroMQ.        !!!
	!!! READ THE SAFETY SECTION BELOW BEFORE YOU CONNECT A DRIVE.     !!!

This merges two other samples: the core-isolated EtherCAT/ZeroMQ split of
sample/50-ethercatzmq (see that sample's README for the general architecture
rationale) with the raw SOEM/CiA 402 Elmo Platinum drive control of
sample/49-elmoplatinum (see that sample's README for the DS402 state-machine
background). Both of those samples are untouched, unrelated references; this
is a new, separate sample.

Required hardware:

	* A Raspberry Pi with an on-board Ethernet controller (e.g. CM5), which
	  also supports multi-core operation (Raspberry Pi 2/3/4/5)
	* One Elmo Platinum drive, connected to the on-board Ethernet port
	* A USB Ethernet device with a CDC Ethernet (ECM) interface, for
	  instance based on a Realtek RTL815x chip, connected to your network
	  with a DHCP server and reachable from the machine running the ZeroMQ
	  broker. On the Raspberry Pi 5 it has to be attached to a root port
	  directly, because USB 3 hubs are not supported.
	* A serial USB adapter (3.3V level) or a Raspberry Pi Debug Probe, to
	  read the debug output (see "DEBUG OUTPUT" below)

Do not connect the EtherCAT port to your office network. The EtherCAT master
sends raw Ethernet frames to this port and there is no IP stack behind it.


BEFORE YOU START: SAFETY

Unlike sample/49-elmoplatinum (which runs a small, bounded demo move
automatically once "Operation enabled" is reached), this sample never
commands any motion on its own, and the drive is **disarmed by default at
boot**:

	* The EtherCAT bus is always brought up to OPERATIONAL automatically
	  (process data has to flow either way), but the drive itself is held
	  in the CiA 402 "Ready to switch on" state (controlword Shutdown,
	  0x0006) until an explicit `{"cmd":"start"}` is received over ZeroMQ.
	* "start" arms the drive: it runs the CiA 402 enable ladder up to
	  "Operation enabled" (power stage enabled), but the target velocity
	  is always written as 0 - there is no velocity-command message in
	  this sample, so no motion is ever actually commanded.
	* "stop" disarms the drive: it writes the standard DS402 "Shutdown"
	  controlword (0x0006), landing the drive back in "Ready to switch
	  on" (power stage disabled, motor de-energized/coasting). This is
	  **not** the distinct DS402 "Quick Stop" function (which needs CoE
	  objects 0x605A/0x6085 configured and a different controlword bit
	  pattern) - it is a plain, unconditional disable, not a
	  safety-rated or ramped stop. Do not rely on it as an emergency stop.

Even though target velocity is always 0, an armed drive ("Operation
enabled") is a live power stage: depending on your motor/drive
configuration it may actively hold position/resist being turned by hand.
Treat "armed" as "the motor could move" at all times.

There is no watchdog-driven interlock and no velocity-command path in this
sample - "start"/"stop" only arm/disarm the CiA 402 state machine. This
sample has never been tested against real Elmo hardware.


BUILD

This sample requires multi-core support, which is not enabled in a default
build of Circle. Reconfigure the toolchain first (repeat any options you
already used, e.g. -r for the Raspberry Pi model, --prefix, ... - see
"./configure --help"):

	./configure --multicore [your other options]

This adds "DEFINE += -DARM_ALLOW_MULTI_CORE" to Config.mk. It affects every
sample/app you build afterwards, not just this one.

Download and build the SOEM library, as described in the file
addon/soem/README:

	cd addon/soem
	git clone https://github.com/OpenEtherCATsociety/SOEM.git
	make

This also builds the OSAL patch (already applied in this repository), which
makes SOEM's OSAL layer (osal.cpp) aware of which core it is running on -
see sample/50-ethercatzmq/README's BUILD section for why this is needed. No
further change to osal.cpp is required for this sample: ecx_SDOwrite()/
ecx_SDOread() (used heavily by ConfigureDrive() below) go through the same
already-patched OSAL path as ecx_config_init()/ecx_statecheck().

Then build this sample as usual:

	cd sample/51-elmoplatinumzmq
	make

If the library libsoem.a has not been built, the build of this sample is
skipped with a message, so that a build of all samples does not fail.


WHAT IT DOES

* The on-board Ethernet device (net device 0, "eth0") is used for EtherCAT.
  The class CElmoPlatinumCore1Master (see elmo_core1.h/.cpp) implements the
  same raw SOEM/CiA 402 bring-up as sample/49-elmoplatinum's
  CElmoPlatinumMaster, but its Run() method is called directly from
  CMultiCoreSupport::Run(1) (see kernel.h/.cpp) instead of being a CTask,
  exactly like sample/50-ethercatzmq's CEtherCATCore1Master:

	1. ecx_init()/ecx_config_init() bring the slave from INIT to PRE_OP.
	2. While in PRE_OP, the slave's identity is read and logged (vendor
	   0x9A / product 0x100002 or 0x01100002 is recognized as an Elmo
	   Platinum drive; other slaves are logged as unrecognized but still
	   configured on a best-effort basis), then ConfigureDrive() writes
	   the CoE/SDO configuration:
		- RxPDO 0x1600: Controlword (0x6040) + Target velocity (0x60FF)
		- TxPDO 0x1A00: Statusword (0x6041) + Velocity actual value (0x606C)
		- PDO assignment via 0x1C12/0x1C13
		- Free Run mode forced on sync managers 2/3 via 0x1C32:01/
		  0x1C33:01 (see TROUBLESHOOTING below)
		- Mode of operation (0x6060) = 3 (Profile Velocity)
		- Conservative motion limits (0x607F, 0x6083, 0x6084)
	3. ecx_config_map_group() maps the process data, driving PRE_OP -> SAFE_OP.
	4. An explicit 5 ms delay (SAFE_OP_TO_OPERATIONAL_DELAY_US), then the
	   bus is unconditionally requested to OPERATIONAL - this happens
	   regardless of whether the drive is armed, because reaching
	   OPERATIONAL is a bus-level (AL state) concept, while arming is a
	   drive-level (CiA 402) concept driven independently every cycle.
	5. Once OPERATIONAL, the cyclic loop decodes the drive's statusword
	   into its CiA 402 state and drives the controlword every cycle,
	   gated by the current armed/disarmed intent (see below).

  Cross-core touch points are limited to GetStatus() and SubmitCommand(),
  both CSpinLock-protected, same pattern as sample/50-ethercatzmq.

* The second Ethernet device (net device 1, "eth1") runs a TCP/IP stack on
  core 0. CZeroMQClient (see zmqclient.h) is a CTask which connects to a
  ZeroMQ broker, publishes a JSON status message every 500 ms (see
  ZMQ_STATUS_INTERVAL_MS in kernel.h), and receives Start (arm) / Stop
  (disarm) commands and forwards them to CElmoPlatinumCore1Master::
  SubmitCommand().

The CiA 402 controlword decision each cycle (elmo_core1.cpp,
CyclicProcess()):

	Fault / Fault reaction active   -> Fault Reset (always, regardless of arm state)
	Not ready / Switch on disabled  -> Shutdown (always - a prerequisite for arming)
	Ready to switch on              -> armed: Switch On   | disarmed: Shutdown (no-op here)
	Switched on                     -> armed: Enable Op.  | disarmed: Shutdown
	Operation enabled                -> armed: Enable Op.  | disarmed: Shutdown (the actual disarm path)
	Quick stop active                -> Shutdown (never commanded by this master)

Target velocity is always written as 0.


THE ZEROMQ PROTOCOL

Same hand-written minimal ZMTP 3.0 client as sample/50-ethercatzmq (see
zmtp.h/.cpp, copied unchanged) - NULL security mechanism only, DEALER role
talking to a ROUTER-based broker (no PUB/SUB support, so it will not
interoperate out of the box with a plain zmq.PUB/zmq.SUB pair or a stock
XPUB/XSUB proxy). Two message "topics" are multiplexed over the one
DEALER<->ROUTER connection, distinguished by the first frame:

	["status",  <JSON>]	sent periodically, device -> broker
	["command", <JSON>]	received asynchronously, broker -> device,
				body is {"cmd":"start"} (arm) or {"cmd":"stop"} (disarm)

The hand-written ZMTP greeting has been checked against RFC 23
(https://rfc.zeromq.org/spec/23/), but was not validated against a packet
capture of a real libzmq peer. Verify it against your actual broker (e.g.
with Wireshark's ZMTP dissector) before relying on it in an important setup.

A minimal Python test broker, which exercises this protocol end-to-end,
including sending Start/Stop from stdin (requires "pip install pyzmq"):

	import sys, threading, zmq, json

	ctx = zmq.Context()
	sock = ctx.socket(zmq.ROUTER)
	sock.bind("tcp://*:5555")

	lock = threading.Lock()
	last_identity = [None]

	def stdin_loop():
	    for line in sys.stdin:
	        cmd = line.strip().lower()
	        if cmd not in ("start", "stop"):
	            print("type 'start' or 'stop'")
	            continue
	        with lock:
	            identity = last_identity[0]
	        if identity is None:
	            print("no client connected yet")
	            continue
	        sock.send_multipart(
	            [identity, b"command", json.dumps({"cmd": cmd}).encode()])
	        print("sent", cmd)

	threading.Thread(target=stdin_loop, daemon=True).start()

	while True:
	    identity, msgtype, payload = sock.recv_multipart()
	    with lock:
	        last_identity[0] = identity
	    if msgtype == b"status":
	        print(json.loads(payload))


CONFIGURATION

The used net devices, the cycle time and the ZeroMQ broker address are
defined in kernel.h:

	ELMO_NET_DEVICE		net device for the Elmo Platinum drive (default 0)
	ZMQ_NET_DEVICE			net device for the ZeroMQ client (default 1)
	ELMO_CYCLE_TIME_US		process data cycle time (default 4000 us)
	ZMQ_BROKER_HOST_DEFAULT	default ZeroMQ broker host
	ZMQ_BROKER_PORT_DEFAULT	default ZeroMQ broker port (default 5555)
	ZMQ_STATUS_INTERVAL_MS		interval of the status messages (default 500 ms)

The broker address can be overridden without rebuilding, using these options
in cmdline.txt on the SD card:

	zmqbroker=192.168.1.10		ZeroMQ broker host name or IP address
	zmqport=5555			ZeroMQ broker port

Drive-tuning constants are defined in elmo_core1.h, matching where
sample/49-elmoplatinum keeps them:

	ELMO_MAX_PROFILE_VELOCITY	max Profile Velocity, object 0x607F (default 5000 counts/s)
	ELMO_PROFILE_ACCEL/DECEL	object 0x6083/0x6084 (default 10000 counts/s^2)
	SAFE_OP_TO_OPERATIONAL_DELAY_US	delay before requesting OPERATIONAL (default 5000 us)


DEBUG OUTPUT

The debug messages (the Circle system log) are written to the serial
interface. On the Raspberry Pi 5 the dedicated 3-pin UART connector is used,
served by uart10 (serial device 10 in Circle, "ttyS11"). Other Raspberry Pi
models use the UART on GPIO14/15. Settings: 115200 baud, 8 data bits, no
parity, 1 stop bit, defined in kernel.h (SERIAL_DEVICE_NUMBER and
SERIAL_BAUD_RATE).

The following options can be added to cmdline.txt on the SD card:

	loglevel=4	log level: 0 (panic) to 4 (debug, this is the default)
	logtarget=tty1	write the log messages to the screen (HDMI) instead
	logtarget=ttyS11 write them to the UART connector (this is the default)

The output looks like this (boot, no command sent yet - the drive stays
disarmed, matching sample/49-elmoplatinum's transcript up through "Ready to
switch on" but not beyond, and without any demo motion):

	00:00:02.14 kernel: Compile time: Aug 19 2026 09:12:33
	00:00:02.14 kernel: 2 net device(s) available
	00:00:02.15 elmo: Elmo Platinum master starting on eth0 (cycle time 4000 us)
	00:00:02.31 elmo: 1 slave(s) found, slave 1 is in PRE_OP, configuring the drive
	00:00:02.31 elmo: slave[1] "EPD" vendor 0x9A product 0x100002: Elmo Platinum drive
	00:00:02.33 elmo: Forced Free Run mode (no Distributed Clocks) on sync managers 2 and 3
	00:00:02.35 elmo: Drive configured: Profile Velocity mode, max velocity 5000 counts/s
	00:00:02.40 elmo: Slave reached SAFE_OP (6 byte(s) out, 6 byte(s) in, expected WKC 3)
	00:00:02.40 elmo: Waiting 5000 us before requesting OPERATIONAL
	00:00:02.60 elmo: Slave is operational
	00:00:02.60 elmo: Drive state: Switch on disabled (statusword 0x0250, disarmed)
	00:00:02.61 elmo: Drive state: Ready to switch on (statusword 0x0231, disarmed)
	00:00:04.01 zmqclient: Connected to broker 192.168.1.10:5555
	00:00:12.15 kernel: EtherCAT: OPERATIONAL, drive: Ready to switch on, WKC 3 of 3, ..., disarmed

Sending {"cmd":"start"} (type "start" in the test broker above) shows:

	00:01:00.00 zmqclient: Received Start command (arm)
	00:01:00.00 elmo: Start requested, arming the drive
	00:01:00.00 elmo: Drive state: Switched on (statusword 0x0233, armed)
	00:01:00.01 elmo: Drive state: Operation enabled (statusword 0x0237, armed)

...and the status JSON's "drivestate" field becomes "Operation enabled",
"armed" becomes true, "targetvelocity" stays 0 - no motion is commanded.

Sending {"cmd":"stop"} shows the reverse:

	00:01:30.00 zmqclient: Received Stop command (disarm)
	00:01:30.00 elmo: Stop requested, disarming the drive
	00:01:30.00 elmo: Drive state: Ready to switch on (statusword 0x0231, disarmed)

To see the internal debug messages of SOEM too, build the SOEM library with:

	cd addon/soem
	make clean
	make EC_DEBUG=1


TROUBLESHOOTING

If the bus reaches SAFE_OP but fails to reach OPERATIONAL, the log shows the
EtherCAT AL status code and its string, e.g.:

	elmo: Slave not operational (state 0x04, AL status 0x002D: DC Sync1 Cycle Time)

AL status 0x2D ("DC Sync1 Cycle Time") means the drive's Sync Manager 3
(inputs) was configured by its own default ESI/firmware to synchronize to
Distributed Clocks SYNC1 (CoE object 0x1C33:01 = 0x0003), and no valid Sync1
cycle time was ever programmed by the master. This is a common default on
Elmo drives. This sample avoids the problem instead of solving it "properly",
exactly like sample/49-elmoplatinum: ConfigureDrive() forces both sync
managers into Free Run mode (0x1C32:01 and 0x1C33:01 = 0x0000) while the
slave is still in PRE_OP, so the drive never expects Distributed Clocks in
the first place, and ecx_configdc() is not called at all. This Circle SOEM
port does not generate real SYNC0/SYNC1 hardware pulses and does not
discipline the Raspberry Pi's clock to the DC master clock (see
addon/soem/README, "REQUIREMENTS AND LIMITATIONS"), so genuine DC-synchronous
drive operation is not meaningfully available here anyway; Free Run matches
what CyclicProcess() already does - a free-running software poll loop.

If the Free Run override itself fails (logged as a warning, not a fatal
error, since some firmware may expose 0x1C32/0x1C33 read-only), the drive may
still require Distributed Clocks; check the AL status again to confirm.


NOTES

Core 1 runs the EtherCAT/CiA 402 cyclic loop directly, without the Circle
scheduler: it uses CTimer::SimpleusDelay()/CTimer::Get()->MsDelay() to pace
the cycle and the reconfigure retry, instead of CScheduler::usSleep()/
MsSleep() (which sample/49-elmoplatinum uses, since it runs on core 0). Core
1 is the exclusive owner of the SOEM context, the on-board net device and
the process data image, so it needs no locking for those. Only its status
snapshot (read by the ZeroMQ client) and its command slot (written by the
ZeroMQ client) are shared across cores, and both are protected with
CSpinLock. Do not add any other cross-core sharing to
CElmoPlatinumCore1Master without the same care: CScheduler, CTask, CMutex,
and the CSocket/CNetSubSystem based networking stack are only safe on core 0
(see doc/multicore.txt).

Cycle times below one millisecond are not recommended, same as for
sample/49-elmoplatinum/47-ethercatweb. There is no synchronization of the
system time of the Raspberry Pi to the distributed clock of the EtherCAT
network.

If no slaves are found or the Ethernet link is down, the master scans the
bus again every five seconds; the armed/disarmed intent (m_bRunning) is not
reset by a rescan, so the drive comes back up in the same arm state it was
requested to be in before the rescan. If the connection to the broker is
lost, the ZeroMQ client retries every five seconds.

This sample assumes a single slave (slave 1) on the bus and does not scan
for or identify multiple drives, matching sample/49-elmoplatinum; unlike
sample/50-ethercatzmq, it does not port that sample's generic multi-slave
CheckSlaveStates() recovery logic - a lost connection triggers a full
rescan/reconfigure from PRE_OP instead of a lighter per-slave nudge, which
also re-applies ConfigureDrive()'s SDO writes.
